  "title": "# Build: approvals-in-place-r2 ## Brett said (verbatim) \"How come when I click approve, it's going …",
  "asked": "# Build: approvals-in-place-r2 ## Brett said (verbatim) \"How come when I click approve, it's going back to axHub, not staying on console?\" \"Yep\" (queue the fix). Resume pending item console-approvals-in-place (steps s1-s4 done on branch claude/console-approvals-in-place if it exists; check). Finish it: 1. Approve / Deny in the console pop-up, banner, Follow Amber dialog and AmberX notification act in place through the console relay. Never bounce to axHub, never open a new window or tab. Result shown in place. 2. Same for UI Review approve. 3. Also covers pending item approvals-no-window-spam... step 1 (no new windows). Note it. 4. Tests, report, screenshots to UI Review, PR to the merge queue. Do NOT land directly. One session, lean chain. ## Addendum 1 (Brett, voice) \"Schedule a console build and deploy every night at 2 a.m.\" and \"I need refresh added to the API, the MCP and the CLI too.\" - Nightly: cron on the VPS at 02:00 America/New_York runs deploy/install-console.sh (rebuild + deploy from current main) with the existing flock; log to /root/axf-builds/nightly-deploy.log; banner note on success/failure. Only when main changed since the last deploy. Setting to turn it off / change the time (Claude settings). - New console command 'refresh' (console.refresh in control@1): reloads the live console page(s) (soft reload, or hard reload with cache clear as an option). REST + MCP + CLI + voice. ## Addendum 2 (Brett, voice) - UI Review choices \"Make UI Review have a separate tab under each one for anything that requires a choice. You need to be able to select multiple with priorities. If you select multiple, it will create a separate view for each one you selected, with the first one being default.\" - UI Review items can carry a 'Choices' set: a Choices tab under the item (Screens / Choices / Comments). Builder submits N options (predicted shots). - Brett selects one or several and orders them by priority (drag or number). Result on the item: chosen[] in order. Single pick = build that one. Multiple picks = build each as a selectable view/layout, the first = default. - API/MCP/CLI for submit choices + read the decision; the build waits on the decision like a normal hold. - Also: UI Review grid gets a Date column of its own and sortable columns (Brett asked earlier). ## Addendum 3 (Brett, voice) - every choice becomes an asset \"Not all asset types can have more than one view; a settings interface may not need more than one. But make every choice, even the ones I don't pick, an asset, stored in that plugin's folder as an asset for the library.\" - Multi-pick only offered when the asset type supports multiple views; otherwise single pick. - Every submitted option (chosen or not) is saved as a real asset of its type in the owning plugin's assets folder, tagged with the choice set and whether it was picked, so it shows in the asset library and can be applied later. ## Addendum 4 (Brett, voice) \"Every UI in the Appearance section will allow you to choose from any of the assets that have been made for it.\" - Settings > Appearance: for every UI that has assets (screens, dialogs, settings interfaces, tab groups, banners, menus, badges...), a picker listing every saved asset of that type for that UI (including unpicked UI Review options), with preview, apply, reset to default. Backed by the asset library + UI registry; REST/MCP/CLI + voice. ## Addendum 5 (Brett, voice) \"Everything comes shipped (all options as assets). For UI Review, for everything sent through it, I need a minimum of three choices. A setting for the default number of choices, and an option to enable a prompt any time something is sent, asking me how many options I want built.\" - Rule: every UI Review submission ships at least N options (default N = 3) as predicted shots + saved assets. Setting in UI Review settings: default option count (min 3). - Optional prompt (setting on/off): when a build is about to submit a UI, Brett gets asked how many options to make (console pop-…",
